  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe center of high pressure aloft continues to sit nearby,\nmainly over the Arizona and New Mexico border. This placement\nwill be responsible for this week's above normal temperatures as\nwell as the downturn in thunderstorm activity over the next day\nor so. Precipitable water values have dropped below 0.9 inches \nin the eastern counties near the New Mexico border with greater \nPWats further west, 1.25-1.5 inches from T.O. Nation westward. \nSlight chances (10-20 percent) for a thunderstorm are forecast \ntoday from the higher terrain of Santa Cruz westward through \ncentral Pima county, but otherwise a down day for monsoon \nactivity remains in store. Any thunderstorm that does form today\nwill bring a strong, gusty wind threat given the ample dry sub-\ncloud air and high DCAPE values.\n\nA similar setup is in store on Wednesday as the upper high\nnudges west. Forecast temperatures increase by a degree or two\nfrom today and thunderstorm chances remain minimal to zero,\nmainly contained again to Santa Cruz to central Pima county in\nthe 10-15 percent range.\n\nThe upper high will generally remain in place through the\nremainder of the work week, but some moisture should begin to\nmove back into the region Thursday onwards. Thunderstorm chances\nincrease from 10-30 percent Thursday to a more widespread 20-60\npercent chance daily Friday through the weekend. This weekend\ninto Monday especially has signals for a more active monsoon \npattern as a west coast trough begins to nudge closer and \nprovide some additional upper level support. There remains time \nfor details to be worked out.\n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday as\nforecast high temperatures sit 6 to 10 degrees above normal.\nTuesday's forecast of 108 at the Tucson airport sits right on\nthe record high for the day of 108 set in 2011.",
